## 14. Advantages of XGBoost

- Strong performance on structured datasets.
- Supports classification and regression.
- Includes L1 and L2 regularization.
- Handles missing feature values.
- Uses first-order and second-order gradient information.
- Supports parallel processing during tree construction.
- Provides feature importance information.
- Supports different objective functions.
- Offers flexible hyperparameter tuning.
- Can reduce overfitting through regularization and sampling.

## 15. Limitations of XGBoost

- Can require significant computational resources.
- Hyperparameter tuning can be time-consuming.
- Sequential boosting limits parallelization across boosting rounds.
- Can overfit with excessive tree complexity.
- Less interpretable than simple Decision Trees.
- Requires careful handling of categorical variables depending on the API and configuration.
- May not be the best choice for every dataset.
- Small datasets may not always benefit from a complex boosting model.

